WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.900
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.440
You may have noticed several videos in a row, there's this big...

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.840
empty ar- do you know why that is?

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.380
It's because over here...

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.440
I was making a video for Wizard School, and I- that was my tripod.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:12.540
Ten years, John.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.400
You'd think I'd have bought a second tripod.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.120
There it is.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:15.900
That's better.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.320
So John, I think it is important

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:18.300
for...

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.520
us to talk about the things that we disagree with our old selves about.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.900
So I wanted to talk about this train that I used to be on.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.900
Called the Machiavellian Train.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.880
I don't know why it's a train. I decided just now.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.820
And the idea is that good people

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.760
have to be willing to do all the same stuff that bad people are willing to do in order to get a good outcome,

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.660
because otherwise the bad people are gonna do all their bad stuff,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:40.220
and they're gonna get their bad thing done.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.840
This was a very appealing idea to me as a younger man.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:49.860
Partly, yes, because it allowed me to excuse me doing things that maybe weren't in consort with my values.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.100
But I think it was mostly because it played into this thing that I wanted to believe about myself.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:59.040
That if I could only use my brain enough, I could manipulate other people and the world,

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.680
to an eventual positive outcome.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.960
Like I was the protagonist in a lot of the books that I liked.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:07.640
Like, I was Ender Wiggin, or Frank Chalmers, or Paul Atreides

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.280
If you don't know what those things are, that's fine. We don't read the same kinds of books.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:11.820
As I have gotten more...

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:12.900
power...

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.240
uh, you hear people in social media circles call this "influence,"

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.380
but let's call it what it is.

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.660
I have gotten way more wary, and even, sort of afraid of that perspective.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.160
So I wanna give you some reasons why I think, like, the Machiavelli train

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.600
is made of B.S.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:31.500
So first, healthy societies rely on trust.

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:34.400
And if we are all seeing each other as pawns,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.880
that trust will never exist. And when I

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.200
saw people more as pawns to be moved to my will,

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:43.920
I was able to trust other people less, 'cause I thought they saw me that way too.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.300
And that, among all the other bad stuff that comes with that, is just a bad feeling.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.740
Second, you are never a hundred percent

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.520
that the thing you're trying to accomplish is actually gonna have a positive effect in the world.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.100
There are lots of examples of people having big revolutions against really oppressive things,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.540
and then it getting even worse afterward.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.160
There are also examples of people having revolutions and the outcome being good.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.580
But you never really know. So if you're headed along

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.080
to this eventual goal, and you're just destroying everything on the way there?

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.960
If this turns out not to have the intended effect,

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.260
then you've done a lot of bad stuff to get to a bad outcome.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.680
Third, you gotta remember,

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.320
that there's a really high chance,

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.920
that you're not gonna get to the desired outcome.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.660
You're never gonna get there. So you could do a whole bunch of bad stuff on the way to trying to get there,

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:28.380
and then fail. And that happens...

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.160
all the time!

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.420
And lastly, I have come to realize that this thing...

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.520
doesn't exist.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.200
There is no final end goal. Every single decision along the path of your life

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.840
is an end in itself.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.620
And if the entire world lived as if the ends justify the means,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.680
then the means in themselves

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.560
are gonna be a terrifying end.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.540
So I jumped off that train.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.740
And now I do not see an idea as a good idea

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.920
if it requires me to defy my values or my ethics.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:57.780
It doesn't matter how good it's gonna be in the end,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.120
or what- all the stuff I could do with that money if I got it.

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.920
And I kinda needed to get okay with discarding ideas

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.020
because I couldn't figure out how to do them

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.900
and still be living the right way.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.820
And that was surprisingly hard. Because I think, throughout my life,

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.220
I praised ideas above reality.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.120
In a kind of way. I don't know if that made sense.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.660
And I needed to get over that, and it's kind of been a liberation for me, because, one,

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:22.700
limitations are actually good for ideas. Like, being able to do

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.420
anything to get to your desired goal can be really paralyzing.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.580
And two, it means that when I fail,

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.400
which I will do over and over and over again, and we all will do,

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:35.940
at least along the way to that failure, I did a bunch of things that I believed in.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.340
And honestly, I've come to accept and believe

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:40.540
that it's those millions of tiny,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.080
sometimes invisible actions, more than any huge accomplishment,

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.000
that really makes humanity work.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:49.460
John, in the last ten years of doing this with you, and with this community,

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.080
this is one of the best...

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:53.620
and most helpful,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.660
most important lessons I've learned.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:56.560
Thank you for that.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:57.780
Memento mori.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.160
And I will see you in Bosto-

